==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Flame, not lame-care for.wav|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[attend]] to the needs of, especially in the manner of a [[nurse]] or personal [[aide]].
#: {{syn|en|besee<l:dialectal>|look after|take care of}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''cared for''' my ailing mother for five years.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|chiefly in the negative}} To [[like]] or [[appreciate]]; to consider to be [[appealing]], [[tasteful]], or [[suitable]].
#: {{syn|en|think much of}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=14 Dec 1719|publisher=w:Jonathan Swift|chapter=To Dr. Sheridan|title=The Poems of Jonathan Swift, D.D., Vol. 2|editor=W. E. Browning
|passage=Mrs. Dingley and Mrs. Johnson say, truly they don't '''care for''' your wife's company, though they like your wine.}}
#* {{RQ:Time|date=17 April 2006|author=Unmesh Kher|title=Whale On the Plate|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100404150914/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,1184070-2,00.html
|passage=Still, while most Japanese may not '''care for''' the meat, many object to calls to stop whaling.}}

====Usage notes====
* In the sense of ''like or appreciate'', often used in negative constructions, as in: ''I do not '''care for''' chocolate'' or questions like ''Would you '''care for''' a cucumber sandwich?''

